deep.reconstruction:
Setting. The page belongs to Grothendieck's combinatorial treatment of the stratification of moduli of stable curves, the 'Teichmüller tower' or 'Lego' of the Esquisse d'un programme. An MD-graph G consists of:
- a vertex set S with a genus weight g: S -> N;
- a set of 'half-edges' Â = I ⊔ A⃗, where I are the free legs (marked points) and A⃗ are the oriented edges;
- an origin map o: Â -> S;
- a fixed-point-free involution σ on A⃗, whose orbits are the edges A.
Per vertex, Â_s = o^{-1}(s), with ν_s = #I_s, ν→_s = #A⃗_s, and ν̂_s = ν_s + ν→_s the total valence. Attached objects: M_G is the stratum of curves with dual graph G (rigidified by G); M̂_G is its closure; 𝔛_G and 𝔛̂_G are the universal curves. The previous page treated contraction of a set of edges C (S' = π_0(G_{0C}), genus = virtual genus of each component). This page lists further morphism types.

1) Découpage (cutting σ-orbits) and recollage (gluing pairs of free legs). Geometrically this is partial normalisation versus gluing by identification of points. If G' is obtained from G by cutting:
- S = S', g_s = g'_s and ν̂_s = ν̂'_s, but ν_s and ν→_s change (half-edges become legs);
- M_G ≅ M_{G'} and M̂_G ≅ M̂_{G'} (for the G-rigidified strata);
- the universal curves receive normalisation maps 𝔛_{G'} -> 𝔛_G and 𝔛̂_{G'} -> 𝔛̂_G.
The main use is to express M_G (resp. M̂_G) as a product ∏_{s∈S} M_{g_s, Â_s} (resp. ∏_s M̂_{g_s, Â_s}). This is the standard clutching description of boundary strata: cut all edges to get a disjoint union of one-vertex graphs.

2) 'Ordinary' homomorphisms G -> G'. These are graph maps compatible with the weights (g' ∘ u = g). One gets M_G -> M_{G'} and a comparison of universal curves 𝔛_{G'} ×_{M_{G'}} M_G -> 𝔛_G, but not maps of the compactified strata M̂_G -> M̂_{G'}. The interesting special case is the transition (forgetful) maps between the M_{g,ν}, which 'already do not pass to the compactifications'. Added in the margin: for every s, the map A⃗_s -> A⃗'_{s'} must be injective; such u are called 'immersions'.

3) Induction homomorphisms u: G' -> G, given by u_0: S' -> S and u_1: Â' = I' ⊔ A⃗' -> Â = I ⊔ A⃗, subject to:
- (a) o ∘ u_1 = u_0 ∘ o' (compatible with origin, but not with extremity or with σ globally);
- (b) u_1^{-1}(I) ⊂ I', i.e. u_1(A⃗') ⊂ A⃗, with u_1 commuting with σ, σ' on A⃗';
- (c) u_1 injective on each star Â'_{s'} -> Â_{u_0(s')}.
An abandoned version of (c) required stability of u_1(I'_1) under σ.

This notion simultaneously covers three things:
- cutting (a leg of G' maps to a half-edge of G);
- inclusion of a subgraph or a vertex star (legs of G' go to edges of G);
- forgetting legs (u_1 non-surjective).
It is designed so that a G-curve induces, by restriction to components and forgetting, a G'-curve. This is the 'restriction' functoriality underlying the Teichmüller-tower formalism: contraction (previous page), cutting/gluing, and induction together generate the morphisms of the tower.
